.TH REHAB-CONFIG 5 "" "rehab" "File Formats and Conventions"
.SH NAME
rehab-config \- configuration file format for rehab(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ~/.config/rehab/config.toml
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR rehab (1)
reads an optional configuration file written in TOML. The file defines named
.IR sequences ,
selects the default sequence, and tunes undo-journal rotation. It is entirely
optional: with no config file, rehab uses only its built-in sequences and
defaults.
.PP
Configuration is additive over the built-ins. A sequence defined in the config
whose name matches a built-in replaces that built-in; a sequence with a new
name extends the available set.
.SH FILE DISCOVERY
The configuration file is located using the following order, highest priority
first:
.IP 1. 4
An explicit path given with
.B \-\-config
.RB ( \-f )
on the command line.
.IP 2. 4
.BR ~/.config/rehab/config.toml
(the same path on Linux and macOS). If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set, it overrides the
.B ~/.config
base directory.
.IP 3. 4
No file: only the built-in sequences and defaults apply.
.PP
When an explicit
.B \-\-config
path is given but does not exist, rehab reports an error rather than falling
back to discovery.
.SH FORMAT
The file has three parts, all optional: a top-level
.B default
key, a
.B [sequences.<name>]
table for each named sequence, and a
.B [journal]
table for rotation settings.
.SS Top-level keys
.TP
.B default
.RI ( string )
Name of the sequence to apply when none is given with
.BR \-\-sequence " (" \-s ).
When unset, the built-in
.B default
sequence is used
.RB ( safe " \(-> " wipeup " \(-> " unicode-clean ).
.SS Sequence tables
Each named sequence is declared as a
.B [sequences.<name>]
table.
.TP
.B filters
.RI ( "array of string" ", required)"
The ordered chain of filter names to apply to each filename component. The
output of each filter feeds the next. Valid filter names are
.BR safe ", " wipeup ", " lower ", " unicode-clean ", and " cgi-unescape
(see
.BR FILTERS
below). An unknown filter name is an error when the sequence is resolved.
.TP
.B separator
.RI ( string ", optional)"
Preferred replacement separator for the
.B safe
and
.B wipeup
filters.
.TP
.B on_collision
.RI ( string ", optional)"
Preferred behavior when a target name already exists:
.BR suffix ", " skip ", or " overwrite .
.PP
.B Note:
The per-sequence
.B separator
and
.B on_collision
keys are parsed and validated but are
.I not yet
wired into the rename pipeline. For now the separator is always
.RB \(lq _ \(rq
and the collision policy is selected with the
.B \-\-on-collision
command-line flag. Wiring these keys through is tracked as a follow-up.
.SS Journal table
Undo journals accumulate one file per non-dry-run. The
.B [journal]
table controls their rotation.
.TP
.B keep
.RI ( integer ", optional)"
Number of journals to retain when pruning. A value of
.B 0
means unlimited (never prune). When unset, the default is
.BR 20 .
The keep count precedence is: the
.BR \-\-keep-journals " / " \-\-keep
flag, then this config value, then the default of 20.
.TP
.B auto_prune
.RI ( boolean ", optional)"
When
.BR true ,
prune old journals automatically after each run. Defaults to
.BR false ,
in which case pruning happens only when
.B \-\-prune-journals
is passed.
.SH FILTERS
.TP
.B safe
Replace spaces and shell-problematic characters (for example
.BR "( ) { } [ ] & ; | < > * ? ! ' \(dq $" )
with a separator.
.TP
.B wipeup
Collapse repeated separators and trim leading and trailing separators.
.TP
.B lower
Lowercase the name.
.TP
.B unicode-clean
Strip Unicode control and format characters. See
.BR unicode (7).
.TP
.B cgi-unescape
Decode
.B %XX
percent-escapes, as produced by CGI/URL encoding. See
.BR ascii (7)
and
.BR utf-8 (7).
.SH BUILT-IN SEQUENCES
.TP
.B default
.BR safe " \(-> " wipeup " \(-> " unicode-clean
.TP
.B safe
.B safe
.TP
.B safe-lower
.BR safe " \(-> " wipeup " \(-> " lower
.TP
.B iso8859_1
.BR cgi-unescape " \(-> " safe " \(-> " wipeup " \(-> " unicode-clean
.RB "(see " iso_8859-1 (7))
.TP
.B utf8
.BR cgi-unescape " \(-> " safe " \(-> " wipeup " \(-> " unicode-clean
.RB "(see " utf-8 (7))
.SH EXAMPLES
A configuration that defines a custom sequence, makes it the default, and keeps
only the five most recent journals:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
default = "myseq"

[sequences.myseq]
filters = ["cgi-unescape", "safe", "lower"]
separator = "-"
on_collision = "skip"

[journal]
keep = 5
auto_prune = true
.fi
.RE
.PP
Redefining a built-in sequence (here, adding lowercasing to
.BR safe ):
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[sequences.safe]
filters = ["safe", "lower"]
.fi
.RE
.PP
The command
.B rehab init
writes a commented file mirroring rehab's defaults to the standard location, as
a starting point to edit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.config/rehab/config.toml
Default configuration file (same path on Linux and macOS). Overridden by
.B \-\-config
or by
.BR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Overrides the base directory used to locate the configuration file. When set,
rehab reads
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rehab/config.toml
instead of
.BR ~/.config/rehab/config.toml .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rehab (1),
.BR ascii (7),
.BR iso_8859-1 (7),
.BR unicode (7),
.BR utf-8 (7)
